latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Calculate the aspect ratio (width / height)\n   b. Determine the overall dimensions\n   c. Calculate the density of red squares (number of red squares / total cells)\n   d. Identify significant patterns:\n      - Check for frame-like structures (red squares on edges)\n      - Detect clusters of red squares\n      - Identify any prominent lines or diagonals\n\n2. Determine the output grid size:\n   a. If aspect ratio > 2, set output to 1 row with width = min(7, input_width)\n   b. If aspect ratio < 0.5, set output to 1 column with height = min(5, input_height)\n   c. Otherwise:\n      - Calculate the total number of red squares\n      - If red squares < 10, use a 3x3 grid\n      - If 10 <= red squares < 20, use a 4x4 grid\n      - If red squares >= 20, use a 5x5 grid\n   d. Adjust the output size if necessary to maintain a similar aspect ratio to the input\n\n3. Create an initial abstract pattern:\n   a. Start with an empty output grid\n   b. If the input has a frame-like structure, create a frame in the output\n   c. Identify the most significant cluster or line in the input\n   d. Represent this significant feature in the output grid\n\n4. Refine the pattern:\n   a. Calculate the target number of red squares in the output based on input density\n   b. While the number of red squares in the output is less than the target:\n      - Add red squares to represent secondary clusters or lines from the input\n      - Prioritize adding squares that maintain the general structure of the input pattern\n   c. If the output is still too sparse, add red squares to balance the pattern\n\n5. Adjust for special cases:\n   a. Ensure there is at least one red square in the output\n   b. For very sparse inputs, limit the number of red squares in the output to avoid over-representation\n   c. If the input has clear symmetry, adjust the output to reflect this\n\n6. Fine-tune the pattern:\n   a. Check if any single red squares are isolated (no adjacent red squares)\n   b. If found, either connect them to nearby red squares or remove them to simplify the pattern\n   c. Ensure the corners of the output grid match the general pattern of the input corners\n\n7. Convert the abstract pattern to the final output grid:\n   a. Create a ColoredGrid object with the dimensions determined in step 2\n   b. Fill the grid with the pattern created in steps 3-6, using 2 for red squares and 0 for black squares\n\n8. Validate the output:\n   a. Check that the output grid size matches what was determined in step 2\n   b. Verify that the density of red squares in the output is similar to the input\n   c. Ensure that the output captures the most significant features of the input pattern\n\n9. Return the final ColoredGrid object as the solution\n\nThis plan addresses the key points from our reflection, including better handling of different input sizes and densities, preserving significant patterns, and ensuring a balance between simplification and representation of the input structure. It also includes steps to handle special cases and fine-tune the output, which should help in creating a more accurate and consistent solution across all test cases."
